  "id": "coldwaterfinancial",
  "note": "Coldwater Financial on Marrow Street (917,910). It opened there as Coldwater Financial on 2026-11-22 and was renamed and rebuilt with its portico the same week. It stood at 922,911 as a grey stone strongroom reaching over its own forecourt; that model is in git before this date, and the plot is a vacant_plot now. This one is the Meridian's decade and the Meridian's stone: ty_palais_stone on an ty_hc_granite plinth, columns in ty_marble_col, every pier and fin in bronze (no white trim), ty_meridian_bronze frames and bands, and a ty_verdigris cap.\n\n⚠ IT FACES A CARRIAGEWAY NOW, SO THERE IS NO `keepsReach`. Nothing passes the plot line: the plinth is the widest thing at 0.44 and the bollards stand on it.\n\n⚠ THE PORTICO CARRIES THE FRIEZE. The hall's front wall is set back to y 0.26, and four round marble columns (granite plinth, bronze base ring, tapered sixteen-sided shaft with a gilt band, a flaring deco capital and a bronze abacus) hold up the frieze where it oversails the recess, between two solid end piers. They are segs, not a pilaster rank, because they stand free. The doors and windows are on the recessed wall behind them. Above the frieze a stepped crest over the door leads into the setbacks and the copper cap.\n\n⚠ THE NAME IS CUT DEEP: two bare boards, a pale lip 0.004 below a near-black cut, so it reads incised by day; at night the cut is lit warm from inside and three pools wash the frieze.\n\n⚠ ITS TRADE IS `reserve_bank`, NOT `bank`, so KIT_DECLINE can keep the whole derived kit off it without touching Loan Wolf. The kit hung sills across the glazed screen and a trade blade on it. Never `noKit` on an authored model: that drops the model's own detail too.\n\n⚠ THE RECESSED WALL IS ONE SCREEN of bronze-framed glass, pier to pier, with the doors in the middle and a window over them. No stone shows between the bays.\n\n⚠ THE NAME IS CUT INTO THE FRIEZE (`bare`, the `deco` hand, gilt) and nothing is hung off the building, as the Meridian does it. The second line is the tile's `sign_trade`, never a second board.\n\n⚠ NO `neon` KEY, for the reason the old model gave: `derivedStyle` reads it as a truth test and dresses the building as a shopfront. `trade` says it is a bank without claiming to replace the bank arm.\n\n⚠ THE ROOMS FIT IT: a full-height banking hall behind the three front windows, the mantrap and the safe deposit gallery in a line south of it, and the vault under the gallery. The setbacks are not floors anybody can reach.",
  "pal": "ty_palais_stone",
